UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 34, Lesson 4: Peer feedback', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Peer feedback should be kind, specific and useful — naming what worked and one clear improvement without unkind jokes.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nRewrite unkind \"This is boring\" into useful feedback on a legend ending.\n\nFEEDBACK\nUseful: \"I lost the memory link at the end — can you name the place again in the last line?\"\n\nCHECK\nVague praise alone does not help a writer improve.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nSwap stories with a peer or parent. Give two strengths and one improvement using the kind/specific/useful pattern. Write the feedback you received and one change you will make.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Peer feedback should be kind, specific and useful — naming what worked and one clear improvement without unkind jokes.","lesson_number":4,"lesson_role":"Speaking and handwriting","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN2-OLC-01","EN2-HANDW-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Swap stories with a peer or parent. Give two strengths and one improvement using the kind/specific/useful pattern. Write the feedback you received and one change you will make. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Useful: \"I lost the memory link at the end — can you name the place again in the last line?\" Reasoning: I used peer feedback by attending to kind and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Vague praise alone does not help a writer improve. Correction: Useful: \"I lost the memory link at the end — can you name the place again in the last line?\" Why: the correction restores accurate use of peer feedback.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Vague praise alone does not help a writer improve. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating peer feedback on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with peer feedback, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Useful: \"I lost the memory link at the end — can you name the place again in the last line?\" This matches the lesson skill for peer feedback.","options":["Useful: \"I lost the memory link at the end — can you name the place again in the last line?\"","Vague praise alone does not help a writer improve.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work."],"question":"Rewrite unkind \"This is boring\" into useful feedback on a legend ending. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Vague praise alone does not help a writer improve. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Useful: \"I lost the memory link at the end — can you name the place again in the last line?\"","Careful use of kind and explain why it matters with clear evidence.","Vague praise alone does not help a writer improve.","Peer feedback should be kind, specific and useful — naming what worked and one clear improvement without unkind jokes."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising peer feedback?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named kind and explain why it matters, not by copying the model.","options":["Treat a missing detail as a proven fact.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the kind and explain why it matters used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right."],"question":"A learner is practising peer feedback. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries peer feedback.","options":["Point to exact words in the sentence that show peer feedback and say what they do.","Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event.","Say the writing is good without naming any words.","Vague praise alone does not help a writer improve."],"question":"Read: \"At dawn, Mina whispered to her goat as frost glittered on the fence.\" Which choice best uses peer feedback from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill; the others miss the evidence.","options":["Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Useful: \"I lost the memory link at the end — can you name the place again in the last line?\"","Vague praise alone does not help a writer improve.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer."],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about peer feedback?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect.","options":["Treat a missing detail as a proven fact.","Locate a specific word, phrase or structural clue before deciding.","Guess from the title alone with no textual clue.","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged."],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to practise kind and explain why it matters?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding.","options":["Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length.","Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea.","Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake.","Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess."],"question":"Which revision actually repairs meaning?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice.","options":["Change fonts and claim the work is new.","Vague praise alone does not help a writer improve.","Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about peer feedback.","Retype the worked model as the final answer."],"question":"Which response shows transfer of peer feedback to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating peer feedback on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Useful: \"I lost the memory link at the end — can you name the place again in the last line?\" Reasoning: I used peer feedback by attending to kind and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with peer feedback, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Vague praise alone does not help a writer improve. Correction: Useful: \"I lost the memory link at the end — can you name the place again in the last line?\" Why: the correction restores accurate use of peer feedback.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-eng-y3-w34-l4","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise peer feedback.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use peer feedback with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify kind and explain why it matters.","I can practise specific independently on a new prompt.","I can notice useful needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Peer feedback should be kind, specific and useful — naming what worked and one clear improvement without unkind jokes. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A guess with no explanation.","Peer feedback should be kind, specific and useful — naming what worked and one clear improvement without unkind jokes.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about peer feedback?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\n\"Your place link was clear when you named the oval gum. Next, slow down on the storm sentence so we catch the threat.\" Kind + specific + useful.\n\nTRY FIRST\nRewrite unkind \"This is boring\" into useful feedback on a legend ending.\n\nFEEDBACK\nUseful: \"I lost the memory link at the end — can you name the place again in the last line?\"","explain":"Peer feedback should be kind, specific and useful — naming what worked and one clear improvement without unkind jokes.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Vague praise alone does not help a writer improve. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Feedback that is kind, specific and useful.","caption":"Name what worked and one improvement.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Kind + specific</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">place link clear: oval gum</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Useful next</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">slow on the storm sentence</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: \"Your place link was clear when you named the oval gum. Next, slow down on the storm sentence so we catch the threat.\" K Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: \"Your place link was clear when you named the oval gum. Next, slow down on the storm sentence so we catch the threat.\" K Then write your own attempt."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation.","Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.","A claim unrelated to the text."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about peer feedback?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\n\"Your place link was clear when you named the oval gum. Next, slow down on the storm sentence so we catch the threat.\" Kind + specific + useful. Think about how this word or relationship creates meaning.\n\nTRY FIRST\nUnderline or quote one precise detail in the model and explain its effect.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning.","explain":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Saying a choice is good without naming the actual detail does not show understanding. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Explain the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Useful ending note contrasted with unkind vagueness.","caption":"Help the writer see a next step.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Rewrite</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">name the place again in the last line</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Unhelpful</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">This is boring</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation.","Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about peer feedback?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nVague praise alone does not help a writer improve. Here is a more productive model: \"Your place link was clear when you named the oval gum. Next, slow down on the storm sentence so we catch the threat.\" Kind + specific + useful.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach would weaken your work, then write a corrected version.\n\nFEEDBACK\nUseful: \"I lost the memory link at the end — can you name the place again in the last line?\"","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"A superficial change in length or appearance does not repair meaning. Vague praise alone does not help a writer improve. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation.","Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about peer feedback?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nPeer feedback should be kind, specific and useful — naming what worked and one clear improvement without unkind jokes. Now choose different people, facts or text to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake one fresh example demonstrating the skill and explain your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should meet the same purpose as the model, but contain original material.","explain":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new content. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Transfer to an independent example"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 34, Lesson 4: Peer feedback","unit_number":7,"unit_title":"Myths, legends and First Nations stories","week":34,"week_in_unit":4,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\n\"Your place link was clear when you named the oval gum. Next, slow down on the storm sentence so we catch the threat.\" Kind + specific + useful.\n\nExplain what makes this example accurate and effective, identifying its specific wording.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Feedback that is kind, specific and useful.","caption":"Name what worked and one improvement.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"1